questions.answer:
function debounce(fn, delay = 300) {
  let timer = null;

  const debounced = (...args) => {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => {
      timer = null;
      fn(...args);
    }, delay);
  };

  debounced.cancel = () => {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = null;
  };

  return debounced;
}

const search = debounce((q) => console.log("search", q), 300);
search("k");
search("ka");
search("kakade");
// after 300ms of quiet: search kakade

Every call clears the previous timer and schedules a new one, so only the final call survives the quiet period. The args are captured in the closure and forwarded with spread. cancel() is important for cleanup on unmount or when a request is superseded; without it, a pending callback can fire after the component is gone. This is a trailing-edge debounce; a leading-edge variant runs immediately and then ignores calls for the delay. For search, pair it with AbortController so an in-flight request is cancelled when a newer query arrives.

