questions.answer:
function throttle(fn, wait = 200) {
  let last = 0;

  return (...args) => {
    const now = Date.now();
    if (now - last >= wait) {
      last = now;
      fn(...args);
    }
  };
}

const onScroll = throttle(() => console.log(scrollY), 200);
window.addEventListener("scroll", onScroll);
// at most one log every 200ms while scrolling

The first call runs immediately because last starts at 0, then calls inside the window are dropped. A trailing-edge throttle schedules one final call at the end of the interval so the last event is not lost; many production helpers support both with options { leading: true, trailing: true }. Use Date.now() or performance.now(); performance.now() is monotonic and immune to clock changes. Throttle is right for scroll, mousemove and drag, where you want steady updates. Debounce is right when only the final value matters, such as search input.

questions.question:
This component leaks memory every time it mounts. Why, and how do you fix it?

function mount() {
  const data = new Array(1000000).fill("x");
  window.addEventListener("resize", () => console.log(data.length));
  setInterval(() => console.log(data.length), 1000);
}

questions.answer:
const io = new IntersectionObserver(
  (entries, observer) => {
    for (const entry of entries) {
      if (!entry.isIntersecting) continue;
      const img = entry.target;
      img.src = img.dataset.src;
      img.removeAttribute("data-src");
      observer.unobserve(img);
    }
  },
  { root: null, rootMargin: "200px", threshold: 0.01 }
);

document.querySelectorAll("img[data-src]").forEach((img) => io.observe(img));

The observer callback receives entries and the observer instance. Check isIntersecting before doing work, copy data-src into src, then unobserve so the image is not processed again. rootMargin: "200px" expands the root box, so loading starts before the image is visible. Keep width and height attributes in the HTML to reserve space and avoid layout shift. If the image fails, add an error handler that sets a placeholder; otherwise the broken state is permanent. Disconnect the observer when the gallery unmounts, and do not lazy-load the hero image because it delays LCP.
